# Agent Rule

## Information

Search for information using the internet or MCP (context7) only when:

- you need to confirm current API or library behavior,
- the task involves security, legal, deployment, or another area where recency is important, or
- the user explicitly asks you to search.

For simple codebase questions and internal implementation work, search the existing code first.

## Response

After responding, cross-verify the provided information again using the internet or MCP (context7) only for high-risk changes or answers that depend on current information.

## Revision

Clearly verify and fix TypeScript issues, lint problems, and import issues. When making modifications, search for the latest information using the internet or MCP (context7) only when current API/library behavior, security, legal, deployment, or another recency-sensitive concern matters.
Reference the package.json version information rather than using the latest versions.

## Code Writing

Always search for and follow the same patterns as existing components, storybooks, etc. when writing new code.
This guideline emphasizes maintaining consistency by referencing and following established patterns in the codebase, whether you're creating new components, writing storybooks, or implementing other types of code.
